 way (aᵢⱼ = −1 for a tail node, 1 for a head","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"node); a directed graph’s edge is the column with one source and one target, the matrix of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"spectral graph theory (B·Bᵀ = L, Godsil–Royle §8.3) and NetworkX’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"incidence_matrix(oriented=True)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". It conforms to ","type":"text"},{"code":"DirectedHypergraph","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", so the ","type":"text"},{"code":"Hypergraphs","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"algorithms (B- and F-connection, components, the clique expansion) run on it unchanged; it is","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"not a ","type":"text"},{"code":"DirectedGraph","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"title":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/documentation\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/OrientedIncidenceMatrix","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol"}}}